// SL-323, RULED by the user Sep 20 and flipped by design 275 U2: a body whose
// ONLY suspension cause is an `any Trait` dispatch is NOT framed, because a
// dispatch owns no park.
//
// The cause is CONSERVATIVE: a vtable word carries no effect, and design 223
// unit 3 refuses the dispatch outright when some conformance implements the
// method with a really-suspending body — which is what makes the move sound,
// since the only dispatches that reach the framing question are ones whose
// every implementation is sync. The two predicates design 275 U4 replaced
// disagreed about it and always had (design 206's executor question struck it,
// SL-306's framing question did not), and the transform framed on the second.
// `via_existential` was therefore pulled into the driven closure, and design
// 88's fence then refused its erased parameter for a frame it never needed:
//
//   error: coroutine transform: `g: &any Greeter` cannot be a parameter of the
//          suspending function `via_existential`. A reference that spans a
//          suspension is held in the frame as a handle to its referent ...
//
// So an ordinary erased-parameter helper could not be called from a driven body
// at all. Nothing here suspends but `root`'s own `__saw_suspend`, and the
// directives below say so: `via_existential` is an ordinary call and gets no
// frame. The dispatch still refuses a `sync` body — "not provably
// suspension-free" is `might_suspend`'s question and the move does not touch
// it (`examples/errors/any_sync_suspend.saw`).
//
// EXPECT: success
// EXPECT-IR-ABSENT: __Frame_via_existential
// EXPECT-IR-CONTAINS: __Frame_root
// EXPECT-OUTPUT:
// 6

trait Greeter {
    func greet(&self) -> Int
}

struct A { n: Int }

extension A: Greeter {
    func greet(&self) -> Int { self.n }
}

func via_existential(g: &any Greeter) -> Int {
    g.greet()
}

func root() -> Int {
    let a = A(n: 5)
    let x = via_existential(&a)
    __saw_suspend()
    x + 1
}

func main() {
    print(__saw_drive(root()))
}
